<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Courland and Semigallia</h1><p class="st-meta">1561 CE – 1795 CE · capital Mitau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Baltic duchy of the Kettler dynasty under Polish overlordship, famous for Duke Jacob's colonies in Africa and the Caribbean.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-order">Livonian Order</a> dissolved in 1561, its last master, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotthard-kettler">Gotthard Kettler</a>, secularised the Order's lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a> and became hereditary Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/semigallians">Semigallia</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. The duchy's capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelgava">Mitau</a> (Jelgava).</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Lutheran Baltic German nobility dominated the duchy, holding Latvian peasants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, and the dukes often clashed with them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-kettler">Duke Jacob Kettler</a> (1642-1682) built ironworks, shipyards and a merchant fleet, and founded colonies at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/courland-gambia">Courland Gambia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/courland-tobago">Courland Tobago</a>. In the 18th century Russian influence grew; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-johann-von-biron">Ernst Johann von Biron</a>, favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anna-of-russia">Empress Anna</a>, became duke in 1737 and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rundale-palace">Rundāle Palace</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Wars, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, ruined the duchy's prosperity. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partition of Poland</a> in 1795, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> nobility submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>, and the duchy was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/governorate-of-courland">Governorate of Courland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Courland and Semigallia" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Courland_(state).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1561 CE – 1795 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mitau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gotthard Kettler, Jacob Kettler, Ernst Johann von Biron</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Latvian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Courland, Kurland, Kurzeme</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia" aria-label="Duchy of Courland and Semigallia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5193 253 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Courland and Semigallia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5513 509L5467 507M5513 509L5556 562M5513 509L5457 693M5513 509L6487 457M5513 509L5513 509"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5467 507h0M5556 562h0M5457 693h0M6487 457h0M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5513 509h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5513 509h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1678&amp;focus=duchy-of-courland-and-semigallia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
